+# Decision: Deliver Committed Changes over Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard browsers need to reflect committed stall-status changes during active weekend markets. The service must support up to 800 simultaneous browsers, opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, recovery after disconnections of up to 20 minutes, and seven days of revision history. Commands already use HTTPS and produce one authoritative event revision per committed edit; this decision concerns how those revisions reach browsers.
+
+The delivery mechanism shapes three components together. The change store must expose enough ordered history for recovery, delivery must move committed revisions to connected browsers, and browser sync must detect interruption and restore a continuous revision sequence. The mechanism must not make connectivity loss look like an unchanged board.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Reliable recovery is a gate.** A browser disconnected for up to 20 minutes must be able to catch up to the authoritative board without accepting gaps or treating stale state as current.
+- **Freshness is major.** Connected staff benefit from seeing operational changes quickly, especially during opening-time bursts. The formal target is within 15 seconds, but lower routine latency improves coordination.
+- **Provider independence is major.** The board should not depend on a hosted realtime provider's subscription protocol, credentials, pricing, or continued availability when the service can reasonably operate the required scale itself.
+- **Operational burden is major.** Persistent connections introduce capacity planning, connection lifecycle management, deployment coordination, observability, and reconnect failure modes.
+- **Recurring infrastructure cost is moderate.** The selected mechanism should avoid unnecessary repeated reads or per-message provider charges at the expected scale.
+- **Implementation complexity is moderate.** Store, delivery, and browser behavior should have explicit boundaries and a recovery model that can be tested without relying on timing alone.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+**Description.** Each browser periodically requests changes after its latest revision. The server remains request/response oriented, and the change store serves cursor-based reads. Failed requests are retried by the browser, with a snapshot available when incremental history cannot satisfy the cursor.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Passes reliable recovery.** Revision cursors and snapshots can restore the board after a disconnection.
+- **Partial freshness fit.** Routine updates arrive after up to one polling interval plus request latency, although five seconds remains inside the formal 15-second target.
+- **Strong provider-independence fit.** Delivery uses ordinary HTTPS without a realtime provider.
+- **Strong operational-burden fit.** Stateless requests avoid persistent connection lifecycle and reconnect orchestration on the server.
+- **Partial recurring-cost fit.** Every connected browser generates repeated reads even when no changes occur.
+- **Strong implementation-complexity fit.** Cursor requests and browser polling are comparatively simple and use familiar request/response behavior.
+
+**Summary.** Polling minimizes connection operations at the cost of repeated idle reads and avoidable update lag.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The service maintains WebSocket sessions with connected browsers and publishes each committed event revision. The change store retains replayable revisions. A browser records its latest applied revision and, after reconnecting, catches up missing revisions before resuming the live stream.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Passes reliable recovery.** Revision-based replay and snapshot fallback can restore a continuous authoritative view independently of the lifetime of a socket session.
+- **Strong freshness fit.** Committed changes can be pushed immediately rather than waiting for the next browser request.
+- **Strong provider-independence fit.** The application owns its wire contract, publication behavior, and recovery model.
+- **Weak operational-burden fit.** The team owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, deployment disruption, reconnect behavior, and publication observability.
+- **Strong recurring-cost fit.** The service avoids both continuous idle polling and hosted realtime subscription fees, while accepting its own connection infrastructure costs.
+- **Partial implementation-complexity fit.** Publication ordering, reconnect, replay, and race-free transition from catch-up to live delivery require coordinated component designs.
+
+**Summary.** Self-hosted WebSockets provide fast, provider-independent updates in exchange for owning persistent-connection and recovery operations.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+**Description.** The application publishes committed revisions through a managed realtime provider. Browsers use the provider's subscription client and reconnect behavior, while application-owned reconciliation ensures that committed store revisions are eventually published and recoverable.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Passes reliable recovery.** Application revision history and reconciliation can recover gaps that provider delivery alone does not cover.
+- **Strong freshness fit.** Managed subscriptions push updates with low routine latency.
+- **Weak provider-independence fit.** Browser integration, credentials, limits, pricing, and delivery semantics depend on the selected provider.
+- **Strong operational-burden fit.** The provider operates connection capacity and much of the session lifecycle.
+- **Partial recurring-cost fit.** Managed operations reduce internal connection work but introduce subscription and usage fees that grow with traffic and connections.
+- **Strong implementation-complexity fit.** Provider SDKs accelerate live delivery, though reliable publication still requires reconciliation with the authoritative store.
+
+**Summary.** Hosted realtime minimizes connection operations and speeds delivery implementation at the cost of recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+MarketBoard uses self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery. This option best combines low-latency updates with provider independence and avoids both repeated idle polling and a recurring realtime-service dependency. The expected scale of 800 simultaneous browsers makes owning connection capacity a bounded trade rather than an open-ended platform commitment.
+
+The decision accepts higher operational and implementation burden. Delivery must manage persistent sessions and expose their health; the browser must reconnect and make connectivity failures visible; and recovery must use authoritative revisions rather than assume that a socket stream is complete. The change store must retain replayable revisions, and the transition from catch-up to live delivery must not lose or reorder committed changes.
+
+This selection turns on the authoritative-revision and visible-connectivity-failure tenets. It rejects five-second polling despite that option meeting the formal freshness target because immediate push better serves active coordination and avoids repeated reads. It rejects hosted realtime despite its lower connection burden because provider independence and avoiding recurring vendor fees outweigh that operational convenience at the expected scale.
